  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}v-\\frac{1}{6}w=2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}v+\\frac{1}{5}w=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$v=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,w=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $6$ and the second equation by $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}v-\\frac{1}{6}w=2 & |\\times 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}v+\\frac{1}{5}w=2 & |\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}4v-w=12 \\\\ 2v+w=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+w & =10 \\\\ w & =10-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $w=10-2v$ into the first equation and then solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4v-w & =12 \\\\ 4v-(10-2v) & =12 \\\\ 4v-10+2v & =12 \\\\ 6v & =22 \\\\ v & =\\frac{22}{6} \\\\ v & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $w,$ we substitute $v=\\frac{11}{3}$ into the equation $w=10-2v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =10-2v\\text{\u00a0} \\\\  & =10-2(\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{22}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-22}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $v=\\frac{11}{3}$ and $w=\\frac{8}{3}.$",
